WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.270
Hi, guys.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:14.610
So we’re going to talk about the brain today.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:19.200
Last lecture I talked to you a little bit
about construct validation and what it means

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.220
to measure something and so, we’re going
to take a bit of a detour.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:32.360
So I told you about measurement problems last
time.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.590
If you do your measurement properly in psychometrics—if
you’re studying personality, if you’re

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:43.830
studying individual differences—there are
roughly six things that you can discover and

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.050
they can be broken up different ways.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:53.410
That’s the big five model: extraversion,
neuroticism, agreeableness, contentiousness,

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.840
openness, and they can be subdivided as you
should know because, in principle, you’ve

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.070
read the paper that subdivided them into ten.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:06.330
That turns out to be quite useful, but for
now we’ll just presume that there are five,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:12.829
and then the other factor of human function
that’s pretty easy to derive and measure

00:01:12.829 --> 00:01:14.259
is IQ.

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:20.460
Now, IQ likely nests under openness because
openness looks like it’s something roughly

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.250
like intellect which is interest in ideas,
but—that’s probably an ability with ideas—but

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.140
that’s probably measured better by an IQ
test, so if I ask you how smart you are and

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.670
you say you’re pretty smart that would give
you a relatively high score on the intellect

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:38.590
scale, but if I tested your IQ I will get
a better measure than that so in some sense

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.799
IQ nests inside the big five model.

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:45.869
But you can think about it as a separate thing
and I think it’s reasonable to do that so,

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:50.329
you know, it depends on what you’re up to
fundamentally.

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:56.650
Now, when the big five was first invented—invented
or discovered, depending on how you look at

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:02.289
it—as I mentioned briefly, as well, it was
brute force statistics basically, so the idea

00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:08.580
behind the big five is that the—it’s called
the lexical hypothesis—and the lexical hypothesis

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:15.250
is that, because human traits are so important
to us because we want to understand ourselves

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.760
and, perhaps even more importantly, we want
to understand other people, and because we

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.360
have language, there’s a reasonable probability
that we’ve encapsulated the structure of

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.640
human personality within our language.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:35.890
And so, if you analyze the linguistic representations
properly, then perhaps you can extract out

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:40.550
the central personality features, and that’s
basically what the lexical hypothesis is.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.690
And it’s important to know the lexical hypotheses.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:49.269
And it’s a bit more sophisticated than that
because it basically presumes that you can

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:55.970
extract out relevant information about personality
from adjectives—descriptive adjectives—from

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:02.319
phrases and from questions, and you’ll get
the same basic dimensional structure regardless

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:04.650
of which of those techniques you use.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:11.010
And that turns out to be true for all intents
and purposes.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:17.330
Now, the lexical hypothesis depends on its
validity, depends for its validity on the

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.959
initial claim that personality is accurately
represented within the structure of language.

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:28.430
And so one of the things I would say is that
there’s a weak big five hypothesis and a

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.970
strong big five hypothesis, okay?

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:38.459
The weak big five hypothesis is that, insofar
as you use language to describe personality,

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:40.420
personality has a five factor structure that’s
reliably extractable.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:48.569
Now the strong big five theory is that, no
no, personality has a five factor structure,

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:52.910
and it’s accurately represented in language,
but when you pull out the structure from language,

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:59.250
what you’re pulling out is actually representative
of genuine personality.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:04.750
Now, the weak hypothesis, I would say, is
established.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:10.270
And it’s worth knowing because what it means
is that if you invent a questionnaire that

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:14.410
has anything to do with self description,
it might be self description at multiple different

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:22.160
levels of analysis, like values for example,
instead of just adjectives, or attitudes instead

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.980
of just adjectives, I mean, these categories
are sort of unclear.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.090
What’s the difference between your attitude
and your temperament?

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:35.490
Well, it’s usually defined by the sentence
that you use the words in, right, you extract

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:41.890
out the words, they sort of stand there alone—it’
s very difficult to clarify their meaning.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:50.100
Anyways, the lexical hypothesis I think is
one of the most well established—it’s

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.440
one of the most well-validated claims in psychology.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.890
I think it may be the most validated claim,
apart from maybe the work that has extracted

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.350
IQ out from cognitive questions.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:09.190
And basically it was brute force statistics,
you know, people developed different lists

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:13.330
of adjectives and you can complain in some
ways about how they did it, and we’ll talk

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.960
about that a bit later, but, because they
tried to throw out evaluative words like good

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:23.560
and evil for example—those aren’t in the
big five—so they tried to only use adjectives

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:30.000
that didn’t necessarily seem to be, like,
valued or devalued.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.950
So introvert and extrovert, well, they’re
opposites, but is it better to be an introvert?

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:37.240
Is it better to be an extravert?

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:43.120
Well, it’s not obvious so all the words
that cover that dimension can be kept in because

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.940
there’s no value judgments associated with
it and the originators of the big five theory

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.040
wanted to keep it as free of values as possible.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.220
Now that’s already a hypothesis right?

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:58.870
It’s a hypothesis that you can come up with
an accurate description of human personality

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:03.000
even if you eliminate all the words that are
clearly valenced.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.420
And we did an experiment here a while back
that was pretty interesting.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:13.000
We got hold of a data set of descriptive adjectives
and we put all the evaluative words back in.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:22.990
So, like nasty and horrible and awkward and
cruel, and then the good words as well: moral,

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:26.020
pleasant, uplifting, all those things.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.840
And then we had people rate people they liked
and people they didn’t like using that expanded

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:36.640
list and we got a real archetype in some sense
of the hateable person and a tremendous number

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.470
of the words that were used came from the
evaluative dimension.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:44.800
So I think that when you throw out the evaluative
words you actually throw out a lot of what

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.630
people actually use, descriptive words to
describe, because when you like someone or

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:54.960
don’t like someone, if someone’s likeable
or unlikeable that’s a pretty important

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:55.960
phenomena.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:00.340
I mean, you hang around the people you like
generally speaking and you don’t associate

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.970
with the people you don’t like, so it’s
a fundamental choice.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:08.580
But we’ll talk about that later because
there’s reasons that happened and the reasons

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.050
were valid, and it’s not that easy to fix
it.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.090
And besides that, the standard five factor
model has all sorts of utility.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.530
But you need to know what its strengths are
and its limitations are.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:25.300
Now, hypothetically, what you do to draw out
the five factor model was you take, imagine

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:31.060
a set of all descriptive adjectives, okay,
and then you take a random sample of those

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:35.890
descriptive adjectives, so that it’s not
biased—you’re not going to use all of

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:39.580
them because, you know, if you asked someone
to describe themselves on a questionnaire

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:44.370
using all the adjectives we have to describe
personality they’d be there for like a day.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:45.450
And so that’s not every efficient.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.080
And so you want to take a random sample.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.080
Maybe you only sample the words that people
are most likely to use.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.250
So for example if you go online you’ll find
that there are word frequency tables.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:04.650
You know, most of your language, about 500
words covers about 80% of what you say.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:09.940
You know, and I don’t remember what the
lexicon of the typical person is, I think

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:15.050
it’s in the neighbourhood of 3500 words,
but that might be wrong, but there’s like

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.010
750,000 words in the English language.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:21.100
It’s an omnivorous language, it just pulls
words in from all over.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:27.150
And so we hardly use any of those words, and
you know, there’s ones that are used extraordinarily

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:28.370
infrequently.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:32.620
So what you could do is you could look at
the total set of adjectives and then you could

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:38.360
pull out, you know, identify the ones that
are used,, you know, the thousand most frequently

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.509
used words and that’d probably cover 95%
of the territory, and then you can pick out

00:08:42.509 --> 00:08:44.540
a random set of 100.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.259
Then you could administer it to people, and
then you could factor analyze it—you’d

00:08:48.259 --> 00:08:52.829
administer it to lots of people—then you
factor analyze it and you get five relatively

00:08:52.829 --> 00:08:54.709
clear and stable factors.

00:08:54.709 --> 00:09:01.769
And the one that comes out first, it’s the
dominant factor in some sense, or the easiest

00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:05.610
measure or maybe the best represented in language
or something like that, it’s extraversion

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.870
versus introversion.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:12.569
And the extraversion is a positive emotion
dimension.

00:09:12.569 --> 00:09:20.129
And it’s associated with enthusiasm and
assertiveness.

00:09:20.129 --> 00:09:24.100
And assertive people will say, they’ll dominate
the conversation with language.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:28.699
Or, well basically that’s the way to think
about it.

00:09:28.699 --> 00:09:31.941
Enthusiastic people, they’re up for anything,
you know, they’re the ones they’re always

00:09:31.941 --> 00:09:33.629
smiling and telling jokes and having parties
and that sort of thing.

00:09:33.629 --> 00:09:38.519
And generally extroverts really like to be
around people.

00:09:38.519 --> 00:09:42.870
So they’re happy, they’re gregarious,
they’re assertive, they’re enthusiastic,

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:47.589
they’re noisy, they blink more than introverts—now
why that is I have no idea, but apparently

00:09:47.589 --> 00:09:48.880
they do [student laughter].

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:55.300
So if you notice someone blinking a lot you
can assume that they’re extroverts.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:59.399
If they’re not conscientious , you know
there’s this idea of people being impulsive,

00:09:59.399 --> 00:10:04.370
it’s a very badly defined term so when you
hear a psychologist talking about impulsivity

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.660
the first think you could realize is they
don’t know, you don’t know what they’re

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:11.389
talking about because probably there’s no
such thing as impulsivity.

00:10:11.389 --> 00:10:19.499
But extraverted people who are not very conscientious
tend to--that's one of the things you might

00:10:19.499 --> 00:10:21.500
think about as associated with impulsivity.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:26.999
There's all sorts of other things though to,
so--okay there's a positive emption dimension

00:10:26.999 --> 00:10:32.000
and that's extraversion and there's a negative
emotion dimension and that's neuroticism.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.300
And neuroticism seems to break down into something
like withdrawal and volatility.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.209
And volatile people are touchy.

00:10:38.209 --> 00:10:42.850
So, you know, they'll react quite a lot if
something negative happens.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.800
So if you tease them they might get upset
or if you start fighting with them in a sort

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.470
of provocative way instead of sort of calming
everything down, they'll just ramp it up.

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.680
And if there's two of you that're volatile
and you're having a conversation and the conversation

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:00.370
goes a little bit, you know, into unpleasant
territory, you can really get each other going.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:01.889
So, yes?

00:11:01.889 --> 00:11:07.950
Student: "Is it like, in terms of the hero,
like internal states?"

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:08.950
Both.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:09.950
Both, yeah.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:11.950
I mean, that's part of the problem with using
the word emotion because, you know, is an

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:16.439
emotion an internal state or a set of behaviours,
and the answer to that is, as far as we can

00:11:16.439 --> 00:11:17.720
tell it's both.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:25.639
So now, I'm going to start talking about extraversion
and neuroticism, and part of the reason for

00:11:25.639 --> 00:11:30.959
that is because I think the evidence for the
relationship between those traits and the

00:11:30.959 --> 00:11:34.680
way the brain functions is, in some sense,
the clearest.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:40.050
Now, you remember the big five were extracted
out using statistical procedures, right, so

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:45.310
there's no reason to assume necessarily that
the traits would bear any straightforward

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:48.410
relationship to brain function.

00:11:48.410 --> 00:11:52.300
Because they weren't derived from analysis
of brain function.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.570
At least not directly.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:59.520
They were extracted from analysis of linguistic
representations of other people's and your

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:00.899
own behaviour.

00:12:00.899 --> 00:12:06.670
But it turns out that there's quite a concordance
between certain elements of neuropsychological

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:13.519
theory and the positive and negative emotional
traits: extraversion and neuroticism.

00:12:13.519 --> 00:12:18.800
And so what I'm going to do today is, I'm
going to talk to you about how positive and

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:26.420
negative emotion are instantiated in some
sense, neurologically.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:33.600
But I'm going to put that in a broader picture
of brain function because, well first of all,

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:34.600
why not?

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:37.490
And second because a lot of the things I'm
going to tell you about the brain today will

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:42.029
sort of retroactively shed light on some of
the clinical theories we've already talked

00:12:42.029 --> 00:12:43.199
about.

00:12:43.199 --> 00:12:48.720
So, which is nice, you know, because, one
of the ways you find out if something is true,

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:53.420
and this is something we also talked about
during the last lecture, is that it manifests

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:57.029
itself even when you use different measurement
techniques.

00:12:57.029 --> 00:13:02.110
And so you have statistical derivation, you
know, which basically took place, it really

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:07.123
started to accelerate I would say, first in
the 1960s and then in the 1980s--it made a

00:13:07.123 --> 00:13:09.059
big comeback in the 1980s.

00:13:09.059 --> 00:13:11.889
But paralleling that were theories of brain
development.

00:13:11.889 --> 00:13:16.001
And they weren't really--the scientists that
were doing the one sort of research weren't

00:13:16.001 --> 00:13:19.290
really communicating with the scientists that
were doing the other kind of research.

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:20.720
So they were methodologically separate.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.839
They used different techniques as well.

00:13:22.839 --> 00:13:27.480
They weren't talking back and forth much and
they were using different measurement techniques.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.941
So if they both come up with the same thing
then you think, oh well there's probably something

00:13:31.941 --> 00:13:37.490
to that because, you know, the sighted people
discovered it and the people who could hear

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:38.490
discovered it.

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:40.560
It's the same kind of idea.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:49.379
So, and you know, this has been worked out
in a fair bit of detail over the last 30 years

00:13:49.379 --> 00:13:50.930
really.

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:56.630
And so the papers that you're supposed to
read with this, they're difficult papers.

00:13:56.630 --> 00:14:00.939
I believe you're reading Grey, which is a
model of the limbic system and basal ganglia,

00:14:00.939 --> 00:14:03.990
applications to anxiety and schizophrenia.

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:09.140
And I think you have goals and behaviour,
that's the Carver&amp;Scheier paper.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:12.050
I put the Swanson paper in as optional.

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:14.869
That's cerebral hemisphere regulation and
motivated behaviour.

00:14:14.869 --> 00:14:16.529
That is a tough paper.

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:18.230
And so is the Grey paper.

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:25.470
And it's partly because these guys, both of
them, Grey's paper is a distillation of a

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.139
very very thick book that's very very difficult.

00:14:28.139 --> 00:14:29.180
And that's been written twice.

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:32.699
It was published first in 1982 and then again
in 1999.

00:14:32.699 --> 00:14:34.319
It's a brilliant book.

00:14:34.319 --> 00:14:36.970
It's had a tremendous impact on psychology.

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:41.730
Grey was one of those people who, he was so
damned smart it was just painful.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:45.190
His reference list--I don't know how many
papers are in his reference list.

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:46.350
Like 2,000.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:49.670
And one of the really weird things about Grey
that makes him stand out among scientists

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:51.740
is, he actually read all those papers.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:54.550
And more than that, he understood them.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:55.860
And so that's really something.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:15:03.680
And so Grey's book on anxiety--he did a lot
of work on animal behaviour and, I'll tell

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:09.790
you, if you want solid psychological information
about how the brain works, the people you

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:16.550
pay attention to are the people who are studying
animal behaviour and animal brains.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:17.740
They know what they're doing.

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:19.569
They're so methodologically careful.

00:15:19.569 --> 00:15:21.389
They're very very reliable.

00:15:21.389 --> 00:15:25.699
And the human neuropsychologists and the people
that use PET scanners and MRIs and all those

00:15:25.699 --> 00:15:28.540
things, they're way way way behind the animal
researchers.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:32.930
Because the animal researchers can just get
in there at a lot higher resolution and they

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.860
can do much more stringent experiments.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:39.759
And they tend to be more methodologically
careful and more scientifically-minded.

00:15:39.759 --> 00:15:42.339
So anyways, the Grey book is a brilliant book.

00:15:42.339 --> 00:15:45.089
It took me like four months to read that damn
book.

00:15:45.089 --> 00:15:52.079
And I mean I really had to take it apart because
he probably poured 30 years of work into it,

00:15:52.079 --> 00:15:54.420
something like that.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.730
He worked a lot in 30 years.

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:56.730
You know?

00:15:56.730 --> 00:16:00.839
He did a lot of experimentation and he read
a lot and so he built a model of the brain's

00:16:00.839 --> 00:16:07.050
function that was based partly on Russian
neuropsychology, partly on cybernetic theory,

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:13.279
partly on animal behaviour, partly on neuropsychopharmacology,
and so that's the study of how drugs affect

00:16:13.279 --> 00:16:19.499
behaviour and cognition, and then partly from
a higher order conceptual perspective.

00:16:19.499 --> 00:16:23.170
And then he stacked all those things up, one
on top of the other, to make sure that the

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:26.619
picture was the same at every single level
of analysis.

00:16:26.619 --> 00:16:31.360
It's absolutely brilliant work and it took
the social psychologists like--they really

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:36.110
started about Grey, although he published
his book in 1982--they probably started thinking

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.910
Grey seriously in about 2005.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:45.559
So it took 23 years for his book to start
to make its wide-scale entrance in the more

00:16:45.559 --> 00:16:48.050
general psychological community.

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:52.910
Anyways, and so you're going to read this
paper which is a model of the limbic system

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:54.500
and basal ganglia.

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:59.230
And 30 years were distilled into the book
and then that was distilled into this paper.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:02.119
So you know, it's rough going.

00:17:02.119 --> 00:17:08.020
And then the Swanson paper, he's another guy--same
kind of guy--he's a developmental neuroanatomist

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:13.060
and if you're really interested in psychology
I would really recommend reading those papers

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:19.500
because they're hard going, but if you get
it, you have this substrata of knowledge underneath

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:21.050
you that's really firm.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:24.310
And I really like Swanson's paper--the guy's
a genius.

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:29.270
And what's so cool about Swanson's paper is
that, if you talk to human neuropsychologists

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:33.140
you'll find that they're biased toward the
cerebral cortex.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:36.870
And they're sort of, I think there's sort
of an anthropomorphic pride that goes along

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:37.870
with that.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:39.030
It's like, well how are we different from
animals.

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:43.190
Well, you know, we've got thumbs and that's
great--hurray for the thumbs--and we stand

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:47.650
upright, but if you look at us neurologically
the big difference is that we have this tremendously

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:53.840
expanded cerebral cortex, especially the prefrontal
cortex, and so then people think--well because,

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.890
you know, because that's one of the things
that differentiates us from animals and it's

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:02.960
relatively evolutionarily modern, because
the human brain really really developed rapidly

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:08.870
over the last two million years, that it's
like king of the brain in some sense.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:12.270
And it's the place where all the complicated
things happen.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:17.110
And that's just not right, because like, breathing--that's
complicated.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.710
That's why you don't get to control it voluntarily.

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:24.060
And keeping your heart going, that's complicated
too, and so you don't get to have a decision

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:25.060
about that.

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:27.300
Your brain has made that automatic.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:31.630
Like millions of years ago because there's
just no way you're going to get voluntary

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.890
control over something that's that vital.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:40.910
You know, so, assuming that newer brain areas
do more complex things, in some ways I think

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:42.670
that's actually precisely backwards.

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:48.570
It's the old brain areas that've been around
for, you know, a hundred million years, those

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:50.170
things are smart.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:55.650
And that's why they survived for so long and
why they're distributed in so many different

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:56.810
species.

00:18:56.810 --> 00:19:02.240
Your brain is somewhat like a lobster's brain--it's
a lot like a mouse brain.

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:03.480
And it's a lot like a rat brain.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:07.640
And you might think a rat isn't a very good
model of a human, because after all, they're

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:12.650
rats, but one thing I can tell you is that
a rat brain is a way better model of a human

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:17.380
brain than your model of human brain is a
model of the human brain.

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:18.380
You know what I mean?

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:19.990
It's like people think of the brain.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:21.860
Well, they have a dopey model.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:27.880
Use a rat as an example, of course it's off
in some ways because rats can't talk for example,

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:34.000
but there are so many levels of analysis where
you might as well be a rat, you know?

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:35.000
Technically speaking.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.210
The pharmacology for example is very very
similar.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:43.580
The function of the neurotransmitters, the
basic anatomy of the structures.

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:46.580
There's a tremendous amount of overlap.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:52.820
And so you might as well go for the commonalities
among brains before you focus in on the differences,

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:56.530
because in some sense that way you get the
basic architecture right.

00:19:56.530 --> 00:20:02.650
Now one of the things that's really interesting
about Swanson, and he's not a neuropsychologist

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:07.520
and he's not really even a psychologist--he's
a developmental neuroanatomist.

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:12.950
And so, there's lots of ways of chopping up
the brain, you know, to look at it, because

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:14.490
it's a really complicated thing.

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:19.540
It's like saying, well how many ways can you
divide up a city to analyze it.

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:25.320
Well, there's endless number of ways that
you can divide up a city to look at it.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:31.020
It's the same with the brain because it's
infinitely subdividable, in some sense, certainly

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:33.460
all the way down to the molecular structures.

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:38.460
And then even at higher order levels, you
know, because it's so complex and it's hierarchically

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:43.630
structured you can just divide it up in all
sorts of different ways and so, at higher

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:48.180
levels of resolution there isn't even that
much agreement on how to name the parts.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:53.000
Now, what the developmental neuroanatomists
do is that they look at how the brain develops

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.010
in utero over time.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:59.860
Because it's a developing thing that sort
of unfolds like a flower unfolds, you know?

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:05.640
And you can track which part of the early
brain turns into the later brain and you can

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:10.810
get some sense of the--it's like an evolutionary
tree you know?

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:15.520
If these three parts grew out of this part
then you can assume that there's some commonality

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:16.520
between them.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:19.110
And that can also help you figure out how
to divide up the brain.

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:21.540
And so that's one of the things that Swanson
does.

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:25.990
But the other thing he does that I really
like is that, instead of looking at the brain

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:30.780
from top down which is what human neuropsychologists
tend to do because they concentrate on say

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:36.940
language and higher order cognitive functions,
which we think of as complex but you think,

00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:42.080
they're not that damn complex, you know, like
a computer can do mathematics so much better

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:45.090
than you that there's not even any comparison,
you know.

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:50.570
And it won't be very long before a lot of
the things that we think of as abstract cognition

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:52.873
are things that computers are going to be
able to do.

00:21:52.873 --> 00:21:55.350
They just won GO tournament, for example.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:22:00.060
And the computers are now better--even your
PC is better at playing chess than probably

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:01.580
anyone in this room.

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:06.400
And the really high end computers are better
at playing chess than anyone who lives.

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:09.950
And so computers are catching up damn fast,
but you'll notice that they don't do things

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:13.520
like bus tables in restaurants, because it
turns out that's really hard.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:16.220
[Student laughter] Well, I mean think about
it.

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:17.220
It's hard.

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:19.780
You're carrying different sized loads all
the time.

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:22.290
You have to interact socially with people
properly.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:23.940
You have to stay the hell out of the way.

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:28.590
You have to exactly time when you're going
to come to the table.

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:32.210
You have to move extraordinarily efficiently,
especially as the place gets busy.

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:35.900
You know, there's a lot of demands on you
to do that job effectively.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:41.570
And you know, we think about it as a low end
job but it's not automated.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:44.370
The low end jobs are the ones that are going
to be automated first.

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:48.420
And it's not obvious that they're going to
be the things that we associated with low-end

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:49.880
labour.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.190
It's just that--well.

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:57.750
Now, what Swanson did with the nervous system--I
was so happy when I found this paper because--he

00:22:57.750 --> 00:23:01.510
did exactly with the nervous system what Piaget
did with children.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:05.780
He showed how the nervous system builds itself
from the bottom up.

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:10.510
And so Swanson lays right on top of Piaget--it's
lovely.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:15.530
And then the other thing that Swanson does
very very nicely is, he elucidates a lot of

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:18.300
the Freudian presuppositions.

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:23.720
So Freud of course talked about the id, and
the id is this, it's sort of like the archaic

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:25.260
brain in some sense, you know.

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:28.480
It's where all your primal impulses lurk.

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:30.870
And you know he called that one thing the
id.

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:39.760
Well you might say, well is there an id in
the brain and the answer is, well, vaguely.

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:41.620
Vaguely.

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:46.470
The brain does differ in terms of the age
of its subcomponents.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:51.740
And as you go deeper into the brain and closer
to the spine, the brain areas get phylogenetically

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:56.060
older, and the more phylogenetically older
areas of the brain, which are underneath the

00:23:56.060 --> 00:24:00.010
cortex, they have their own little circuits.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:04.610
And there's some commonality between them,
and they do govern primal--I think of them

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:06.880
as primal sub-personalities.

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:08.580
We've talked about that before.

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:13.330
It's better than thinking about them as drives
or goals or anything like that because not

00:24:13.330 --> 00:24:21.420
only do they--they're like sets of associated
behaviours, but they're not just that.

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:25.910
They're also ways of looking at the world
and ways of thinking about the world.

00:24:25.910 --> 00:24:32.070
So, you know, if you're angry, it's not like
you're being driven to use your fists.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:33.070
That's not how it works.

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:37.680
What happens is that the angry subpersonality
comes out and, you know, it does a whole bunch

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:38.680
of things.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:41.330
It's got its own state of arousal.

00:24:41.330 --> 00:24:45.590
It's characterized both by positive and negative
emotion, which is something that's quite peculiar

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:51.830
about anger, because it's both a defensive
action and an aggressive, forward moving behaviour.

00:24:51.830 --> 00:24:53.290
And it has a viewpoint.

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:54.610
Like it's a biased viewpoint.

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:58.380
If you're angry with someone, all you can
see about them is how damn irritating they

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:00.620
are and how much they need to be defeated.

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:01.660
You know, roughly speaking.

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:04.170
And so it's a whole personality.

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:09.560
Now, it's a one dimensional personality because
all it's really got is anger, and that's not

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:12.130
enough to solve every problem.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:17.580
But you can't think about it as a drive exactly
because it's not deterministic.

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:21.920
You know, even when you're angry, you have
a range of choices to make.

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:28.340
Now, they're circumscribed by the fact that
you're angry, but it's best to think about

00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:29.690
it as a sub personality.

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:31.600
I think you just get a lot farther that way.

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:36.930
Now, what Swanson did--you know how Piaget
talked about children/infants, they start

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:41.380
out with a few fundamental reflexes, right,
that they're sort of born with, including

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:47.140
the ability to use their mouth and their tongue
as an exploratory device.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:50.800
And then they have certain reflexes that they
can manifest right away, and then they have

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:53.500
the ability to modify those reflexes.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:56.980
And so, as far as Piaget was concerned, that
was enough to get the ball rolling.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:26:00.080
It was sort of like the child was like booting
up across time.

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:05.890
It's got some, you know, outside of the operating
system you've got some bare processes in your

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:09.560
computer that get it started enough so the
operating system can turn on.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:12.400
It sort of unfolds up into something complex.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.840
Well that's sort of what human beings do across
time.

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:19.640
They start out with these basic reflexes that
are simple movements and then they earn how

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:23.570
to modify the movements and then they learn
how to chain the movements together and then

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:31.280
they learn how to use the chained movements
as tools, in some sense, as embodied tools,

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:37.980
to allow these biologically determined sub-personalities
to operate in the world with some chance of

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:38.980
success.

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:43.980
Okay, now you can layer that onto the nervous
system, so part of what's happening when you

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:48.350
get these basic movements down, the ones that
are not automatic like your ability to grip

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:54.560
and the ability to do this with your hands
and all the basic subroutines you have with

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:58.560
your body--those are automatized, they're
made into little machines, and a lot of them

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:01.360
run spinally.

00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:05.310
You know, because what you think in some sense
is that, if you're walking, you're thinking

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.640
about walking in some way--that's how you
walk.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:08.930
And it's like, that's not right.

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:14.080
What happens is your orient yourself to a
goal, and in some sense you just disinhibit

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:16.410
the system that knows how to walk.

00:27:16.410 --> 00:27:19.860
And then it more or less does it automatically,
and you know, you direct it.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:22.880
And you can modify what it's doing if there's
an error.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:24.220
But you kind of know that, right?

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:29.130
You drive to school or you drive somewhere
you're familiar to, you don't even necessarily

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:32.160
remember the drive if you've done it 50 times.

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:37.160
It's because you're basically--in some sense
you're just sequencing automated behaviours.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:42.530
And, a good thing too because, if you didn't
have those automated expert behaviours at

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.780
hand, you wouldn't know how to drive.

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:51.870
Because, you're just not smart--the time that
you drive most consciously is when you first

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:53.080
learn how to drive.

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:59.390
Well, you don't want a conscious driver, you
want an unconscious expert driver whose consciousness

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:02.290
is monitoring the world for anything unexpected.

00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:03.290
That's what you want.

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:06.160
So, and that's the case with almost everything
you do.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:10.120
If you have to become conscious of what you're
doing, the probability that you don't do it

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:11.790
very well is really high.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:15.560
And you know that because maybe you're talking
to someone, it happens when I'm lecturing

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:20.290
sometimes--if I'm not really on top of things
I'll get self-conscious--and that's a terrible

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:23.580
thing to have happen when you lecture, you
know, it just makes you stumble.

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:28.450
If it's going well I know where I'm going
and I have all these little routines that're

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:31.700
stories and things that I know that--they
just automatically sequence.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:36.750
All I have to do is point the lecture in the
right direction and, you know, the things

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:43.910
that are logically associated with the direction
of the lecture will--they'll come up.

00:28:43.910 --> 00:28:45.940
They're primed in some sense.

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:52.290
And so, Swanson helped me understand the functions
of the lower part of the brain quite well

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:53.970
and then to map that onto Piaget.

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:59.090
And one of the things he helped me understand
was just exactly how sophisticated the lower

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:02.090
parts of the brain are, the more automatic
parts.

00:29:02.090 --> 00:29:07.140
Now, the behaviourists used to believe that,
or did believe and still believe to some degree,

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:10.270
that a lot of your behaviour is reflexive.

00:29:10.270 --> 00:29:15.330
And the radical behaviourists, the guys from
the 50s, more or less made that case that

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:21.190
even complex behaviour among animals is nothing
but the chaining together of automatic reflexes.

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:24.630
So it was a deterministic chain of reflexes.

00:29:24.630 --> 00:29:30.410
Well, you do have reflex actions, you know,
so if you touch a hot stove you'll jerk your

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:34.860
hand away, and your brain isn't involved in
that--it's just your spinal cord that's involved

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:35.860
in that.

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:39.930
Now, your brain is involved when the pain
hits, and maybe that's so you stop doing it

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:45.780
in the future, but the conscious part of your
brain is too slow to get your hand away from

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:47.080
the stove in time.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:51.770
And so, what's happening in your body in some
sense is that you have these multiple layers

00:29:51.770 --> 00:29:57.630
of neurological organization and some of them
are quite fixed--they're automatic deterministic

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:01.680
systems and those are things you've really
really practiced and that you can just can

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:03.470
run as a routine.

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:07.470
And above that are those things that you've
chained together, but they can be chained

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:10.380
together in different ways so they're less
automatized.

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:15.770
And then if you keep going up they get more
and more complex and less automatized, until

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:21.320
at the top, which is where your consciousness
is, roughly speaking, you have to program

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:22.720
what you're doing, you know.

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:27.370
You have to pay very careful attention to
how to go about doing this because you don't

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:29.640
have the systems built into you.

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:33.060
So you know what that's like if you're trying
to learn something new, it takes a lot of

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:36.240
energy and you really have to pay attention.

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:43.130
And then once you start to get it, well, what
really happens is there's things you don't

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:44.820
have to think about any more.

00:30:44.820 --> 00:30:49.320
And thank god for that, because first of all,
you can't think that much because you're a

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:55.130
limited capacity processor, and second, the
programming is quite difficult.

00:30:55.130 --> 00:31:22.980
Okay so 

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:27.740
if you look on the right there, there's a
little diagram that's a hierarchical--or a

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:31.700
little table really and it's talking about
the motor system.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:37.570
Now the motor system is what allows you to
output action.

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:41.770
And you're outputting action at all sorts
of levels that you don't even realize because,

00:31:41.770 --> 00:31:47.840
you know, you might think that sight is a
sensory activity, for example, but there's

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:51.060
a very tight relationship between sensory
activity and motor activity.

00:31:51.060 --> 00:31:54.730
So, for example, when you're looking at a
scene, even though you don't know it, your

00:31:54.730 --> 00:31:56.850
eyes are going like this.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:32:00.510
And the reason you're going like that is because
if you held your eyes absolutely still, and

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:04.160
you can actually learn to do this, if you
hold your eyes absolutely still your vision

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:06.600
will fade right out and you won't be able
to see anything.

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:12.490
And it's because the little retinal cells
that are picking up the light get exhausted

00:32:12.490 --> 00:32:13.960
and then so they stop transmitting.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:18.050
And so part of the reason you're zipping your
eyes back and forth is so that different parts

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:21.520
of your eye can process the scene and it doesn't
get exhausted.

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:26.930
And plus, you're also looking around and you're
looking at when things move, you know, you

00:32:26.930 --> 00:32:29.560
direct your attention to that, or if someone
makes a gesture.

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:35.720
And so, you're actually kind of using your
eyes like tentacles, you know, to check out

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:39.060
the information that's coded in the light.

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:43.150
And even when you hear and that sort of thing
there's a fair bit of motor activity associated

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:46.910
with it--you turn your head unconsciously
and there's all sorts of little motor actions

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:51.300
in your ear that also help you tune in, and
so those things are tightly associated.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:56.820
But roughly speaking, the motor output is
action.

00:32:56.820 --> 00:33:02.860
And then Swanson shows that there are these
layers in the nervous system that go from

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:05.080
the spinal cord upward.

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:09.520
The first ones are the somatomotor neuron
pools, and the second are locomotor pattern

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:15.620
generators, and the third are locomotor pattern
initiators and then the forth are locomotor

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:16.730
pattern controllers.

00:33:16.730 --> 00:33:17.750
It's a hierarchy.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:23.440
And so, the way to kind of lay Piaget on it,
in some sense, is to think of the somatomotor

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:30.520
neuron pools as simple actions that can be
implemented automatically.

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:35.620
And then the locomotor pattern generator has
taken those simple actions and chained them

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:44.300
into relatively more complex melodies of action,
so kinetic melodies, and then the locomotor

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:49.160
pattern initiator more or less decides when
those kenetic melodies should be implemented.

00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:52.350
And even that can be unconscious.

00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:56.510
So it's still at that level of brain organization,
it's still more or less automatic.

00:33:56.510 --> 00:33:59.140
Even stimulus-response is a way to think about
it.

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:04.170
And then you go one level higher and that's
when you get the ability to voluntarily use

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:06.280
these automated things.

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:09.109
So I can show you an example of how this works.

00:34:09.109 --> 00:34:11.129
This is called a ballistic movement.

00:34:11.129 --> 00:34:15.599
So, I'm going to take my hand and I'm going
to bring it down really fast right toward

00:34:15.599 --> 00:34:16.929
that edge.

00:34:16.929 --> 00:34:21.999
Okay so--alright now, so that's very fast
movement.

00:34:21.999 --> 00:34:27.460
Now one of the things that's cool about that
movement is that I don't have time, once that

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:34.909
action starts, it happens so fast that it's
done by the time that the information about

00:34:34.909 --> 00:34:38.200
that action actually gets to my brain.

00:34:38.200 --> 00:34:42.059
Once I let that go, I can't control it.

00:34:42.059 --> 00:34:49.139
What I basically am doing is disinhibiting
the motor control systems that I already have

00:34:49.139 --> 00:34:53.500
established in my psychophysiological being.

00:34:53.500 --> 00:34:57.500
I'm disinhibiting one of those and then it
runs automatically.

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:02.940
And so that's what you're doing all the time
when you're acting in life.

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:07.369
It's kind of a neat--I wrote a paper a while
back with an undergraduate student of mine,

00:35:07.369 --> 00:35:12.440
he was a pretty bright character, and we got
invited to do a paper--I got invited to do

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:18.279
a paper--on free will, and so we were thinking
about free will and, it's very interesting

00:35:18.279 --> 00:35:22.059
to think about free will in relationship to
these hierarchies because it sort of goes

00:35:22.059 --> 00:35:27.150
like this: it's that, out there in the future,
you've got free will.

00:35:27.150 --> 00:35:31.359
But as the future moves towards the present,
you don't.

00:35:31.359 --> 00:35:36.190
Once it gets close enough to the present you
have to have initiated something automatic

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:37.190
that will happen.

00:35:37.190 --> 00:35:39.319
And so I can give you an example.

00:35:39.319 --> 00:35:45.249
People who play the piano very well, they
look ahead of where they're playing in the

00:35:45.249 --> 00:35:46.249
music.

00:35:46.249 --> 00:35:49.190
Because they know how to do this, they don't
have to think about it.

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:52.589
What they have to do is look ahead so that
the part of them that knows how to do this

00:35:52.589 --> 00:35:54.059
gets ready to do it.

00:35:54.059 --> 00:35:59.470
And all of you are experts in that way likely--all
of those of you who drive--you're also experts

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:00.470
that way.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:01.859
Because when you're driving, where do you
look?

00:36:01.859 --> 00:36:07.650
If you're a good driver, on the highway, you
look at least half a second or a second ahead.

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:08.650
Why?

00:36:08.650 --> 00:36:12.330
Well because 50 feet ahead has already happened.

00:36:12.330 --> 00:36:16.410
Right, it's too close in time for you to do
anything about it, so basically what you're

00:36:16.410 --> 00:36:23.289
doing is you're looking at the road and then
you're disinhibiting sequences of actions--the

00:36:23.289 --> 00:36:28.000
ones that are going to correspond to what
you want to do with the road most adequately.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:34.030
And the closer you get to executing one of
those motor behaviours, the more automated

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:35.030
it is.

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:38.769
SO by the time you manifest the action, there's
no free will there at all.

00:36:38.769 --> 00:36:42.150
But you set it up to be released in some sense.

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:48.690
So I can say, well, there's no free will in
that, but there was in deciding to do it at

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:50.160
that point.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:55.220
So and that's sort of how your automaticity
meets your freedom.

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:58.569
You've got to think about it as something
extending across time.

00:36:58.569 --> 00:37:11.440
Okay, so having established that then we're
going to get into the neurological localization

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:12.720
a little bit.

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:17.880
So you might say, well you collect all these
patterns of behaviour and then you automate

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.089
them into routines.

00:37:19.089 --> 00:37:24.890
I can give you an example, so, for a child,
you know, when a child is first born, if you

00:37:24.890 --> 00:37:26.940
tap on its cheek it'll go like this.

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:30.529
It's called a rooting reflex, and it'll move
its mouth and tongue.

00:37:30.529 --> 00:37:34.490
Well what it’s doing, roughly speaking,
is its searching for a nipple, and if you

00:37:34.490 --> 00:37:38.289
just put your finger there it'll clamp onto
that and start to suck.

00:37:38.289 --> 00:37:41.259
And so the child comes out with that ability.

00:37:41.259 --> 00:37:44.619
And that's a reflex, it's sort of built into
the neurology.

00:37:44.619 --> 00:37:48.430
But then when the child starts to interact
with the mother to actually breastfeed, well

00:37:48.430 --> 00:37:55.519
then that initial reflex has to be modified
quite substantially in the context of the

00:37:55.519 --> 00:37:59.859
relationship with the mother because the bare
reflex, it's enough to get the thing going

00:37:59.859 --> 00:38:01.869
but it's not enough to make it work.

00:38:01.869 --> 00:38:06.559
And so often mothers take a fair bit of time
to learn how to breastfeed, but it's also

00:38:06.559 --> 00:38:09.460
because the baby is taking a fair bit of time
to learn how to do it.

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:13.400
So they have to establish the dance that goes
with that.

00:38:13.400 --> 00:38:18.490
And one of the things that's quite cool about
that dance, so to speak, is there's been studies

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:22.799
done of depressed and non-depressed mothers
interacting with their infants.

00:38:22.799 --> 00:38:27.849
And so if you take a non-depressed mother
interacting with her infant and you just film

00:38:27.849 --> 00:38:33.369
them, and you speed up the film, you can see
the baby reacts and the mother reacts and

00:38:33.369 --> 00:38:35.900
the baby reacts and the mother reacts and
it's like a dance, you know, there's this

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:39.150
continual flow of information between the
two of them.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.309
And if the baby's with a depressed mother
that doesn't happen.

00:38:42.309 --> 00:38:45.230
It's jerky and discontinuous.

00:38:45.230 --> 00:38:50.680
And so if you think about that you can see
how the dialectical interaction between the

00:38:50.680 --> 00:38:58.050
mother and child is building up the child's
repertoire of complex abilities--within a

00:38:58.050 --> 00:38:59.829
social context.

00:38:59.829 --> 00:39:06.289
I know I told you guys before, roughly speaking,
that kids really get socialized to play with

00:39:06.289 --> 00:39:09.529
each other between two and four.

00:39:09.529 --> 00:39:15.749
But that's not exactly true because even at
the lower level, more automated, and more

00:39:15.749 --> 00:39:21.940
primordial reflex behaviours, like the ones
that are associated with breastfeeding, even

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:26.520
those develop under the constraints of social
guidelines.

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:30.930
So society's always there, right from the
reflex level up.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:35.880
But you know what happens when kids are between
two and four, they have to learn to actually

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:41.589
play, they have to learn how to play complex
games with other children and so that involves

00:39:41.589 --> 00:39:45.609
communication and negotiation at a verbal
level.

00:39:45.609 --> 00:39:47.640
And also with people their own age.

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:53.160
So you know, it's continuous in some sense
and discontinuous in another sense.

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:57.930
Okay so anyways, you might say well why do
you bother stringing all these things together,

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:00.349
and well, you think about the breastfeeding
issue.

00:40:00.349 --> 00:40:02.270
Why does a baby bother to learn how to breastfeed?

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:04.359
Well, that's pretty obvious isn't it.

00:40:04.359 --> 00:40:09.029
I mean, first of all, if it doesn’t then
it doesn't live.

00:40:09.029 --> 00:40:13.160
And there's a bunch of reasons for that--one
is obviously it's not going to be nourished

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:17.210
properly, although, there are things you can
do to substitute for that.

00:40:17.210 --> 00:40:21.480
Although still, breastfeeding still seems
to be the best thing you can do for your baby.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:26.410
Although, you can work around it, but fundamentally
it seems to be the best thing you can do.

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:29.880
But then the baby's also learning all sorts
of other things, like it's got skin-to-skin

00:40:29.880 --> 00:40:34.880
contact and it's snuggling and it being comforted
and doing eye gaze to eye gaze and it's cooing

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:39.069
and the mother's imitating it and there's
just a lot of things going on there.

00:40:39.069 --> 00:40:45.589
And so the child is organizing all that, partly
to fulfill a basic biological need, which

00:40:45.589 --> 00:40:50.200
is two needs, both thirst and hunger, but
it’s also fulfilling its need for play,

00:40:50.200 --> 00:40:53.320
for example, because children have a circuit
for play.

00:40:53.320 --> 00:40:58.180
So you could say play is part of the id--it's
a fundamental biological need.

00:40:58.510 --> 00:41:03.850
And Jaak Panksepp was one of the people either
discovered that or worked on it, and that

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:10.839
word was extended by Tiffany Fields in a hospital
in Florida--some really brilliant work.

00:41:10.839 --> 00:41:16.809
She was looking at Panksepp's work on the
necessity of tactile stimulation and she thought,

00:41:16.809 --> 00:41:19.210
hey I bet we could try that with premature
babies.

00:41:19.210 --> 00:41:22.839
Because if a baby's premature, a couple of
things are problems.

00:41:22.839 --> 00:41:27.390
I mean, first of all, at the need of the term,
pregnancy term, that's when babies lay on

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:28.390
fat.

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:33.279
You know, so they come out and they've got
some storage there in case things don't get

00:41:33.279 --> 00:41:34.900
going very well.

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:38.279
And they're gaining a lot of weight in that
last period of pregnancy, so if they come

00:41:38.279 --> 00:41:41.220
out premature then they're really skinny.

00:41:41.220 --> 00:41:44.950
And then what happens often is they start
to lose weight and that's not good because

00:41:44.950 --> 00:41:47.359
they should be ramping up the weight gain
really quickly.

00:41:47.359 --> 00:41:53.180
A newborn baby will outgrow its clothes in
one week, you know, they're gaining weight

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:54.180
quickly.

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:57.910
The premature babies lose weight, and it's
hard to keep them going.

00:41:57.910 --> 00:42:03.529
They put them in an incubator, they feed them,
but what Tiffany Field did was, she had the

00:42:03.529 --> 00:42:10.069
nurses go in there with gloves and massage
the babies for three minutes ten times a day.

00:42:10.069 --> 00:42:14.890
And those babies gained weight as fast as
babies that were still in utero and you could

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:18.470
still detect the differences in their development
six weeks later.

00:42:18.470 --> 00:42:20.690
Which in baby terms is like forever, right.

00:42:20.690 --> 00:42:24.690
I mean you change as much from zero months
to six months as you probably do from six

00:42:24.690 --> 00:42:26.609
months to 20 years old.

00:42:26.609 --> 00:42:29.099
It's such a radical transformation.

00:42:29.099 --> 00:42:35.329
Okay, the reason I'm telling you all this
is because, you lay out these hierarchies

00:42:35.329 --> 00:42:40.869
of behaviour that are well practiced and automatized
and brought under the control of social conditioning,

00:42:40.869 --> 00:42:46.589
but you really do them in the service of--well
that's the question.

00:42:46.589 --> 00:42:50.710
In the service of, we'll say, biological necessity,
for the sake of argument.

00:42:50.710 --> 00:42:55.240
Well you have to eat and you have to drink,
and so one of the reasons you lay out the

00:42:55.240 --> 00:43:00.789
hierarchy that's associated with breastfeeding
is so that you can do that directly, but also

00:43:00.789 --> 00:43:05.230
so that you can establish a relationship so
that not only are you doing it directly this

00:43:05.230 --> 00:43:10.259
day, but you're also going to do it tomorrow
and the next day and the day after that.

00:43:10.259 --> 00:43:17.240
And so the hunger system is integrated with
these other systems of biological constraint

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:22.890
in some sense, and they're trying to come
up with an answer to the continual problem.

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:29.989
So you have dedicated biological systems that
help you address problems that are associated

00:43:29.989 --> 00:43:33.059
problems that are associated with your biological
nature that are universal.

00:43:33.059 --> 00:43:35.589
And those would be the things that Freud would
describe as the id.

00:43:35.589 --> 00:43:43.230
So you can think of these hierarchies of motor
behaviour, which sort of develop up into sub

00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:48.349
personalities, as nested in some sense inside
of motivational states.

00:43:48.349 --> 00:43:53.430
So there's the motorbehavious hierarchy that
you use when you're angry, there's the motorbehaviour

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:59.079
hierarchy you use when you're distressed--and
kids get very good at that, you know, so they'll

00:43:59.079 --> 00:44:05.470
cry, which is a reflex, but by the time they're
about two, they're pretty damn good at crying.

00:44:05.470 --> 00:44:09.259
You know, they've got a cry that sounds like
they're sad and upset when they're really

00:44:09.259 --> 00:44:10.259
angry.

00:44:10.259 --> 00:44:13.170
And so if you really listen you can hear the
difference.

00:44:13.170 --> 00:44:17.200
Because an angry baby sounds different than
a sad baby.

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:21.060
But if you don't listen, the baby will have
you thinking it's sad no problem.

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:24.730
Then you'll feel sorry for it and go help
it out, and really it's just thinking "up

00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:26.319
yours" [Student laughter].

00:44:26.319 --> 00:44:32.369
So we know this partly because--so let's say
you've got a baby and it's under nine months,

00:44:32.369 --> 00:44:34.440
six months old, and it's crying in its crib.

00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:36.009
You think, well what should you do?

00:44:36.009 --> 00:44:39.470
You go in there and you pick up the baby and
you comfort it and you probably do that pretty

00:44:39.470 --> 00:44:43.690
much non-stop when the baby's that young because
they're so young they should really still

00:44:43.690 --> 00:44:45.910
be in utero--they're very young.

00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:49.059
But by nine months that baby's starting to
get smart.

00:44:49.059 --> 00:44:54.650
And so it's about nine months when it can
visually recognize people it knows and people

00:44:54.650 --> 00:44:57.020
he/she doesn’t know.

00:44:57.020 --> 00:45:02.359
And then, so the baby's in there and it's
squawking a bit and so, it wants some attention,

00:45:02.359 --> 00:45:04.809
and then someone walks in and it's not the
mother.

00:45:04.809 --> 00:45:08.869
And the baby bursts into tears and people
think the baby's all sad because its mother

00:45:08.869 --> 00:45:09.869
didn't show up.

00:45:09.869 --> 00:45:14.190
It's not sad, it's really irritated that the
wrong person showed up.

00:45:14.190 --> 00:45:18.839
And you can tell that because people have
done facial coding of the baby's expression

00:45:18.839 --> 00:45:22.049
and also its skin tone and an angry baby looks
angry.

00:45:22.049 --> 00:45:25.380
First of all it turns all red, it's really
angry.

00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:28.680
And you see that behaviour later too in temper
tantrums.

00:45:28.680 --> 00:45:34.519
Like a two year old that's having a temper
tantrum is just having a fit of rage.

00:45:34.519 --> 00:45:39.380
The rage system, because there is a rage system,
is just completely out of control, it's just

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:42.200
absolutely dominating their behaviour.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:47.069
You don't really want to encourage that because
the rage system should start to be modulated

00:45:47.069 --> 00:45:50.759
by other systems and other higher order control
systems.

00:45:50.759 --> 00:45:55.210
Not so much suppressed or repressed which
is sort of how the Freudians would think , but

00:45:55.210 --> 00:46:00.279
integrated into more complex and sophisticated
modes of action, so the child can use its

00:46:00.279 --> 00:46:05.869
anger--which is an extraordinarily useful
capacity, because it allows you to defend

00:46:05.869 --> 00:46:10.069
yourself--so the child can learn to use that
in a sophisticated way so it can solve the

00:46:10.069 --> 00:46:15.059
problems that would lead to a rage attack
without ever getting to the point where it

00:46:15.059 --> 00:46:16.779
has to be enraged.

00:46:16.779 --> 00:46:20.079
That's a functional child--it's not like it's
learned to repress its anger, it's just that

00:46:20.079 --> 00:46:23.059
it gets along with people and never has to
get angry.

00:46:23.059 --> 00:46:30.280
So, now some kids learn to repress their anger
because they're in a household where any manifestation

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:33.289
of anger is dealt with very very harshly.

00:46:33.289 --> 00:46:38.390
And maybe there's a rule that everyone has
to be nice to each other all the time, or

00:46:38.390 --> 00:46:39.390
else.

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:41.549
Sort of a paradoxical rule.

00:46:41.549 --> 00:46:46.119
But anyways, you can imagine there's a lot
of these biological systems and so I want

00:46:46.119 --> 00:46:52.170
to show you some of their instantiation here,
so let's look at this one.

00:46:52.170 --> 00:46:58.369
When you get up above the spinal cord, there's
a little brain area, I'll show you about how

00:46:58.369 --> 00:46:59.640
big it is.

00:46:59.640 --> 00:47:00.640
There it is.

00:47:00.640 --> 00:47:06.540
So there's your brain, the top-on part, the
part on top, that's the cerebral cortex.

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:08.769
That's the part that people are really proud
of.

00:47:08.769 --> 00:47:10.990
It's like, oh wow, look at our cerebral cortex.

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:14.049
Doesn't it have a lot of neurons [student
laughter].

00:47:14.049 --> 00:47:19.520
Then, in the middle there, there are older
brain area--phylogenetically old--and then

00:47:19.520 --> 00:47:22.830
as you go down toward the spinal cord they
basically get older and older and older and

00:47:22.830 --> 00:47:24.499
older.

00:47:24.499 --> 00:47:28.269
And more and more critical to your development.

00:47:28.269 --> 00:47:32.799
So the serotonin system, we talked about it
a little bit in lobsters, right.

00:47:32.799 --> 00:47:37.549
The serotonin system determines where a lobster
is in a dominance hierarchy, it determines

00:47:37.549 --> 00:47:40.829
whether it stands like this or whether it
stands like this.

00:47:40.829 --> 00:47:41.869
That's an old system.

00:47:41.869 --> 00:47:46.609
And when your brain is developing in utero
it's the serotonin system that guides its

00:47:46.609 --> 00:47:47.609
development.

00:47:47.609 --> 00:47:51.200
So that's how important it is, sort of like
the conductor of the orchestra, but it's also

00:47:51.200 --> 00:47:55.960
the thing that controls the entire unfolding
of the brain.

00:47:55.960 --> 00:48:05.200
And it's a really really archaic system, so
in some sense your being is controlled by

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:07.509
these things that are ancient, beyond comprehension.

00:48:07.509 --> 00:48:13.269
Anyways, you look at the hypothalamus and
you think, well that's not very big.

00:48:13.269 --> 00:48:15.430
And yeah, it's not.

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:21.499
But, if you take a cat and you--so a cat brain
looks something like that--if you take off

00:48:21.499 --> 00:48:26.640
all the stuff that's in light orange and most
of the top of the stuff that's above the hypothalamus

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:31.789
that's in white--you just take all that out,
and it's a female cat and it's in a cage--you

00:48:31.789 --> 00:48:36.519
really can't tell the difference between it
and a cat that has all the rest of that brain.

00:48:36.519 --> 00:48:38.690
Now there's some things it can't do.

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:43.579
It doesn't do very well if you take it into
a new situation because it can't learn.

00:48:43.579 --> 00:48:47.050
More importantly in some sense, it can't remember.

00:48:47.050 --> 00:48:51.809
So it's in a perpetual sense of everything
happening for the very first time.

00:48:51.809 --> 00:48:56.440
But it's still capable of a lot of the reactions
that would be necessary for you to stay alive.

00:48:56.440 --> 00:49:01.930
SO it can regulate its liquid intake, it can
regulate its food intake, it can react sexually,

00:49:01.930 --> 00:49:05.989
it's capable of defensive aggression, although
it'll really fly into a rage so it's kind

00:49:05.989 --> 00:49:09.979
of like a disinhibited two year old in some
sense.

00:49:09.979 --> 00:49:14.279
And as long as you keep it in the cage and
provide it with food and water it's like the

00:49:14.279 --> 00:49:16.229
way it lives.

00:49:16.229 --> 00:49:19.989
And it's hyper exploratory which I think is
just weird beyond belief.

00:49:19.989 --> 00:49:23.800
You know, you take the brain off a cat and
then all it wants to do is explore.

00:49:23.800 --> 00:49:25.229
Which is not what you think at all.

00:49:25.229 --> 00:49:30.299
It's like, a cat with no brain is really curious--it
just doesn't make sense.

00:49:30.299 --> 00:49:35.739
But it does actually because the systems that
drive your exploratory urge are rooted in

00:49:35.739 --> 00:49:42.740
that hypothalamus and they're actually the
same systems--it's individual differences

00:49:42.740 --> 00:49:47.380
in the functions and sensitivity of those
systems that are in part associated with individual

00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:49.299
differences in extraversion.

00:49:49.299 --> 00:49:52.920
So extroverts are sort of hyper exploratory
in social situations.

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:54.540
Whereas introverts aren't.

00:49:54.540 --> 00:50:01.420
And the exploratory circuit that has its origins
in the hypothalamus runs on dopamine, roughly

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:06.309
speaking, and then it runs through the base
of your brain and then up into your prefrontal

00:50:06.309 --> 00:50:07.970
cortex in particular.

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:12.660
And it's also the system that responds to
cocaine and amphetamines and other drugs that

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:16.200
people like to take because they heighten
positive emotion.

00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:22.789
So it's variability at a very very low level
in the brain that determines things like the

00:50:22.789 --> 00:50:24.329
differences in extraverts and introverts.

00:50:24.329 --> 00:50:32.010
Anyways, it's this tiny little part of the
brain and if that's all you have you can get

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:35.380
along not too bad.

00:50:35.380 --> 00:50:40.079
But that's also because it's tiny from one
level of analysis, but it's really big from

00:50:40.079 --> 00:50:41.619
another.

00:50:41.619 --> 00:50:48.130
I mean, you can increase your resolution when
you're doing brain analysis all the way down

00:50:48.130 --> 00:50:52.329
to the molecular level and still be looking
at the brain, you know.

00:50:52.329 --> 00:50:57.739
Below the molecular level, at the atomic level,
well everything's made out of atoms.

00:50:57.739 --> 00:51:02.799
But the brain is already there at a molecular
level and it's insanely complicated at a molecular

00:51:02.799 --> 00:51:07.339
level, and the hypothalamus is way bigger
than a molecule.

00:51:07.339 --> 00:51:12.030
And so even though it's small compared to
the rest of our brain it's still a whopping

00:51:12.030 --> 00:51:16.079
big structure by atomic standards and so there's
a hell of a lot going on down there.

00:51:16.079 --> 00:51:22.109
And you notice the hypothalamus, you might
say, is there such a thing as the id.

00:51:22.109 --> 00:51:25.329
Well, yes and no.

00:51:25.329 --> 00:51:26.630
Is it in the brain?

00:51:26.630 --> 00:51:27.979
Yes and no.

00:51:27.979 --> 00:51:33.079
A really low resolution representation of
what's in the brain, that's one way of thinking

00:51:33.079 --> 00:51:34.079
about it.

00:51:34.079 --> 00:51:36.890
But then you might say, is there a hypothalamus?

00:51:36.890 --> 00:51:39.049
And the answer to that is, well, yes and no.

00:51:39.049 --> 00:51:43.309
Because there's the hypothalamic structure,
and you see it's made out of a bunch of things

00:51:43.309 --> 00:51:44.509
that are different.

00:51:44.509 --> 00:51:47.819
And so you could say, well should you even
group those things together.

00:51:47.819 --> 00:51:51.910
And the answer to that is, it depends on why
you're grouping them together.

00:51:51.910 --> 00:51:59.630
If it's useful for some purpose of understanding
or control, to group them together--they're

00:51:59.630 --> 00:52:03.239
similar in the way that they emerge in the
course of brain development, and they're roughly

00:52:03.239 --> 00:52:07.890
of the same phylogenetic age--but there's
lots of reasons to treat them as the same

00:52:07.890 --> 00:52:09.509
and there's lots of reasons to treat them
as different.

00:52:09.509 --> 00:52:12.109
It depends on what you're trying to do.

00:52:12.109 --> 00:52:25.410
Anyways, the hypothalamus has all these little
sub-structures that are really in some sense

00:52:25.410 --> 00:52:29.029
at the core of the sub-personalities that
we talked about.

00:52:29.029 --> 00:52:41.910
So, for example--okay so this is Swanson again
and he's showing you--this is a rat's brain

00:52:41.910 --> 00:52:45.859
from the bottom--and he's showing you the
layout of the hypothalamus.

00:52:45.859 --> 00:52:48.170
You see it's got all those different nuclei.

00:52:48.170 --> 00:52:51.049
It doesn't look like one thing, it looks like
a bunch of things.

00:52:51.049 --> 00:52:59.119
And so here he says, the descending paraventricular
nucleus is involved in the control of eating

00:52:59.119 --> 00:53:02.140
and drinking, and that's shown in green.

00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:06.499
So that's the thing that--when you get hungry
or thirsty--that's the thing that sort of

00:53:06.499 --> 00:53:09.890
turns on your hungry and thirsty sub-personality.

00:53:09.890 --> 00:53:14.269
And then the rest of the cell groups play
a major role in controlling two classes of

00:53:14.269 --> 00:53:18.999
social behaviour, that is, behaviours involving
interactions between animals: reproductive--that's

00:53:18.999 --> 00:53:24.809
the red areas--and defensive--the purplish
areas.

00:53:24.809 --> 00:53:29.460
So now what you can think is, you have all
those little subroutines that we talked about,

00:53:29.460 --> 00:53:34.799
that're sort of at hand and ready to go, and
then they're organized underneath these major

00:53:34.799 --> 00:53:42.359
classes of motivation so that, you have to
do something--like eat--and so it's like there's

00:53:42.359 --> 00:53:46.390
an eating robot in you, that's one way of
thinking about it, except it's smarter than

00:53:46.390 --> 00:53:48.640
that and it's alive and conscious.

00:53:48.640 --> 00:53:54.529
These things are alive, they're not machines
and they're not deterministic.

00:53:54.529 --> 00:53:59.539
You can think of them as low-resolution personalities,
or uni-dimensional personalities because the

00:53:59.539 --> 00:54:02.759
only thing the eating system really cares
about is whether or not you eat.

00:54:02.759 --> 00:54:05.019
And the drinking system is the same thing.

00:54:05.019 --> 00:54:10.039
You know, you see malfunctions sometimes in
people of these singular systems, so you can

00:54:10.039 --> 00:54:16.479
develop a condition if you have hypothalamic
damage where you'll drink water til you die

00:54:16.479 --> 00:54:18.260
and no one can stop you.

00:54:18.260 --> 00:54:22.729
They can lock you in a room away from sinks
and that would stop you, but you'd be raging

00:54:22.729 --> 00:54:26.579
away inside trying to claw through the door
to get to the damn water because you're so

00:54:26.579 --> 00:54:32.469
hyper-thirsty that it's all that there is
left of you in some sense and if you're allowed

00:54:32.469 --> 00:54:37.359
access to a tap, you'll just drink and drink
and drink and drink and drink until you drown.

00:54:37.359 --> 00:54:42.869
And, you know, people can say, hey you've
had enough water--it's like, forget that.

00:54:42.869 --> 00:54:48.089
The hypothalamus is a major league system,
and when push comes to shove, it's the thing

00:54:48.089 --> 00:54:49.539
that's in control.

00:54:49.539 --> 00:54:55.299
And one of the ways of thinking about the
way that you organize your life is that, the

00:54:55.299 --> 00:54:59.190
hypothalamus controls the major motivation
systems--we just talked about those--and so

00:54:59.190 --> 00:55:02.039
those are the things that people would think
about as drives.

00:55:02.039 --> 00:55:05.279
Although they're not drives, they're too complicated
to be drives.

00:55:05.279 --> 00:55:09.819
And then there's other systems that come up
later that we'll also talk about that govern

00:55:09.819 --> 00:55:15.650
emotion, like anxiety and a different system
that governs pain.

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:19.390
Partly what you're trying to do as your organize
your life is to make sure those systems stay

00:55:19.390 --> 00:55:21.160
shut off.

00:55:21.160 --> 00:55:22.809
And then you think, well I'm in control.

00:55:22.809 --> 00:55:24.320
Well no, you're not.

00:55:24.320 --> 00:55:29.549
You just satisfied all the things that could
take control from you, and so now you have

00:55:29.549 --> 00:55:31.089
a modicum of choice.

00:55:31.089 --> 00:55:34.759
And basically what you're doing when all these
things have had what they need is you're running

00:55:34.759 --> 00:55:36.569
around exploring.

00:55:36.569 --> 00:55:41.670
You know, and that's also another one of these
circuits because the hypothalamus, as I already

00:55:41.670 --> 00:55:43.119
said, governs exploration.

00:55:43.119 --> 00:55:52.329
So, a lot of--see, psychologists often get
things backwards and they ask silly questions

00:55:52.329 --> 00:55:54.119
like why are people anxious.

00:55:54.119 --> 00:55:55.769
That's a stupid question.

00:55:55.769 --> 00:56:00.680
It's why are people ever calm--that's a question.

00:56:00.680 --> 00:56:02.269
Why are you anxious?

00:56:02.269 --> 00:56:05.390
Well, hmm, think of all the terrible things
that can happen to you.

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:08.759
And they will happen to you, even worse, and
there isn't anything you can do about it.

00:56:08.759 --> 00:56:12.010
It's like why aren't you quaking in your boots
just sitting there.

00:56:12.010 --> 00:56:14.250
Well, that's a mystery.

00:56:14.250 --> 00:56:21.519
You know, and then, here you are in your normal
state--it's like, well no, this is not a normal

00:56:21.519 --> 00:56:22.519
state.

00:56:22.519 --> 00:56:31.009
People have been trying for seven million
years, as people, to create a situation so

00:56:31.009 --> 00:56:37.579
complex and comfortable that a couple hundred
of you can sit here without having your hypothalamus

00:56:37.579 --> 00:56:42.420
driving you toward something and actually
just listen to something for a few hours.

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:43.869
That's not normal.

00:56:43.869 --> 00:56:48.859
That's so staggeringly abnormal that it's
a miracle.

00:56:48.859 --> 00:56:55.579
The typical person--and for most of human
history--it's like, you are hungry, man.

00:56:55.579 --> 00:56:56.839
And that's not the same.

00:56:56.839 --> 00:57:03.210
Like you people--well some of you have probably
been hungry in your life, but--is there someone

00:57:03.210 --> 00:57:06.950
here who's gone without food for more than
one day?

00:57:06.950 --> 00:57:10.359
Okay has anybody here gone without food for
more than a week.

00:57:10.359 --> 00:57:14.749
Okay, how about three days?

00:57:14.749 --> 00:57:19.520
Okay, so like, are you hungry?

00:57:19.520 --> 00:57:20.520
No.

00:57:20.520 --> 00:57:25.089
You're hungry when you haven't had anything
to eat for twenty days, then you're hungry.

00:57:25.089 --> 00:57:29.650
And the same goes for thirst and cold and
all the things we're just never exposed to.

00:57:29.650 --> 00:57:33.301
So we think civilized people are all calm
and nice and easy to get along with, it's

00:57:33.301 --> 00:57:41.109
like, yeah well you have absolutely everything
you need all the time and that makes you as

00:57:41.109 --> 00:57:45.960
easy to get along with as any completely insane
primate ever gets.

00:57:45.960 --> 00:57:53.049
Okay, so you might say, well god, if you can
survive with just the hypothalamus, what good

00:57:53.049 --> 00:57:55.009
is the rest of the brain?

00:57:55.009 --> 00:58:05.000
And the answer is, well, if you're thirsty--let's
say you're angry, the hunger system isn't

00:58:05.000 --> 00:58:06.730
going to be able to take care of that, right.

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:09.319
So you're hungry, okay, that works.

00:58:09.319 --> 00:58:11.150
You're angry, that works.

00:58:11.150 --> 00:58:16.780
But then you have another problem, which is
you're hungry now and you're angry now, perhaps,

00:58:16.780 --> 00:58:19.059
you can move through those states.

00:58:19.059 --> 00:58:22.210
If you watch that, you'll see that in two
year olds--it's part of why they're so fun

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:23.900
to be around.

00:58:23.900 --> 00:58:27.410
First of all, they're really excited and they're
running around playing and then they trip

00:58:27.410 --> 00:58:30.829
and they're crying like made and then you
poke them and they laugh and then they want

00:58:30.829 --> 00:58:33.140
something to eat and then they fall asleep
[student laughter].

00:58:33.140 --> 00:58:38.200
You know, they're just run by their hypothalamus
and it's really fun to see because they're

00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:42.670
so alive and so enthusiastic and spontaneous,
but they're completely clueless.

00:58:42.670 --> 00:58:47.400
It's like, you take one of those creatures
to the mall and leave it there and it's just

00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:49.059
not happy at all.

00:58:49.059 --> 00:58:54.190
And the reason for that is, well it doesn't
know how to sequence any of its actions, right.

00:58:54.190 --> 00:58:59.890
It runs through these states of need, but
it isn't complicated enough to figure out

00:58:59.890 --> 00:59:06.460
how to address those needs in any consistent
sense across multiple environments in a social

00:59:06.460 --> 00:59:07.460
world.

00:59:07.460 --> 00:59:09.270
You need the rest of your brain for that.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:13.170
And that again fits in with the Piagetian
idea--is that what you're doing is you're

00:59:13.170 --> 00:59:19.119
taking these micropersonalities that are basically
instantiated within the hypothalamus and then

00:59:19.119 --> 00:59:22.430
you're organizing those into more and more
complex games.

00:59:22.430 --> 00:59:27.839
And you need the cortex to do that because
the cortex is partly what enables you to think

00:59:27.839 --> 00:59:29.819
about time.

00:59:29.819 --> 00:59:34.319
You know, because the hypothalamus, it's a
here and now thing.

00:59:34.319 --> 00:59:39.630
The hypothalamic cat lives in the present--there's
the present, and that's it.

00:59:39.630 --> 00:59:44.740
You can probably get some sense of that--like
one of the things that happens to people if

00:59:44.740 --> 00:59:51.460
they take amphetamines or if they take psychedelic
drugs is that some of the inhibitory control,

00:59:51.460 --> 00:59:55.670
some of the more complex control systems that
are cortical, shut down.

00:59:55.670 --> 01:00:01.940
And so then they enter a state where it's
all now and you know, the now just comes at

01:00:01.940 --> 01:00:03.319
people in that state.

01:00:03.319 --> 01:00:09.259
It's a transcendent experience, but of course,
despite that, you're not functional.

01:00:09.259 --> 01:00:15.230
And so in some sense you trade that transcendent
relationship with reality for a narrow functionality.

01:00:15.230 --> 01:00:20.000
And that's too bad and that's life, but it's
a tradeoff.

01:00:20.000 --> 01:00:26.329
So anyways you're organizing these sub-personalities
and you're using your cortex to do that because

01:00:26.329 --> 01:00:31.150
you don't just have to think about eating,
you have to think about, how am I going to

01:00:31.150 --> 01:00:32.270
get the food?

01:00:32.270 --> 01:00:33.680
When is the food going to come?

01:00:33.680 --> 01:00:35.130
How am I going to cook the food?

01:00:35.130 --> 01:00:37.270
What about all the other people that want
to eat?

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:39.479
How do we regulate that in our family?

01:00:39.479 --> 01:00:42.599
And then how do we regulate that with the
fact that we also need to sleep and we need

01:00:42.599 --> 01:00:45.869
to work and we need to get along with other
people and we need to have something interesting

01:00:45.869 --> 01:00:49.479
to do and--it's ridiculously complicated.

01:00:49.479 --> 01:00:57.119
And there's no end to the complication because,
maybe you're just solving the problem for

01:00:57.119 --> 01:00:59.460
you, and then you're two.

01:00:59.460 --> 01:01:01.589
Or maybe you're trying to work it out within
your family.

01:01:01.589 --> 01:01:05.289
Well then that's sort of like the Crumb brothers,
they never get out of their family because

01:01:05.289 --> 01:01:09.809
they never come to an arrangement that works
well enough so that they can, in some sense

01:01:09.809 --> 01:01:11.720
move into the broader world.

01:01:11.720 --> 01:01:16.690
Then you solve the family problem but then
you've got the tribe problem and, maybe you

01:01:16.690 --> 01:01:20.079
can solve that to some degree, but then you
have the broader political problem and it's

01:01:20.079 --> 01:01:25.670
like--there's an infinite problems that crop
up for you to solve so there's plenty for

01:01:25.670 --> 01:01:27.369
you to do with your big cortex.

01:01:27.369 --> 01:01:35.869
And you know we paid a big price for that
thing because once we discovered time, we

01:01:35.869 --> 01:01:39.249
can make a bargain with the future, which
is basically what you do when you regulate

01:01:39.249 --> 01:01:40.359
your behaviour, right.

01:01:40.359 --> 01:01:42.519
You're making a bargain with the future.

01:01:42.519 --> 01:01:50.519
You say to yourself, if I forego so and so
now then, later, it'll pay off.

01:01:50.519 --> 01:01:51.549
And that's real useful.

01:01:51.549 --> 01:01:55.220
You can conceptualize the future, and you
can conceptualize the future as something

01:01:55.220 --> 01:01:56.940
you can bargain with.

01:01:56.940 --> 01:01:58.510
But there's a lot of cost to that.

01:01:58.510 --> 01:02:02.489
One is you don't get to do just what you want
right now, and of course that sucks--it's

01:02:02.489 --> 01:02:05.450
partly why people drink alcohol.

01:02:05.450 --> 01:02:10.190
Because alcohol is disinhibiting, it stops
you from--it actually doesn't stop you from

01:02:10.190 --> 01:02:16.219
thinking about the future--it stops you from
caring about what you think about the future.

01:02:16.219 --> 01:02:17.640
And that's just as good.

01:02:17.640 --> 01:02:22.180
And so then you can go out and have fun because
fun is impulsive and often extremely dangerous,

01:02:22.180 --> 01:02:27.009
because the long term consequences can be
really devastating.

01:02:27.009 --> 01:02:32.700
But it's a real pain to keep yourself controlled
all the time because you're foregoing direct

01:02:32.700 --> 01:02:34.849
reward constantly.

01:02:34.849 --> 01:02:41.079
I think it's part of the reason why people
in heavily industrialized complex countries

01:02:41.079 --> 01:02:43.259
aren't necessarily happy.

01:02:43.259 --> 01:02:48.609
You know, it takes a lot of grinding away
to keep all the machinery that keeps all of

01:02:48.609 --> 01:02:50.470
this in place going.

01:02:50.470 --> 01:02:56.190
So you're not getting typhoid and tigers aren't
tearing you apart, but by the same token,

01:02:56.190 --> 01:03:02.009
you have to be unbelievably disciplined and
ready to act constantly to keep everything

01:03:02.009 --> 01:03:04.079
that's so complex around you working.

01:03:04.079 --> 01:03:06.579
And that's not necessarily fun.

01:03:06.579 --> 01:03:11.819
Okay, now, let’s look at something else.

01:03:11.819 --> 01:03:31.499
Now you can tell me if I showed you this--I
hope I didn't show you this.

01:03:31.499 --> 01:03:32.499
You see that?

01:03:32.499 --> 01:03:33.589
Okay, good.

01:03:33.589 --> 01:03:37.819
Now some of you know this, and so you just
don't say anything.

01:03:37.819 --> 01:03:41.259
Although you probably don't know this specific
version.

01:03:41.259 --> 01:03:45.069
So if you've seen this sort of thing before
you're going to think you're smart while you're

01:03:45.069 --> 01:04:01.819
watching it, but it'll probably still trip
you up--I hope.

01:04:01.819 --> 01:05:44.430
Give me a sec here and we'll see if we can
actually get some sound out of this thing.

01:05:44.430 --> 01:05:54.940
Yeah, so that's quite the illustration.

01:05:54.940 --> 01:06:01.910
So one of the questions you might ask is,
well okay first of all, how many of you didn't

01:06:01.910 --> 01:06:03.599
see the gorilla?

01:06:03.599 --> 01:06:07.119
Okay, how many of you have seen this sort
of video before?

01:06:07.119 --> 01:06:09.109
Okay so you knew about the damn gorilla [student
laughter].

01:06:09.109 --> 01:06:13.920
Of the people who didn't know about the gorilla,
how many people didn't see the gorilla.

01:06:13.920 --> 01:06:15.259
Well, some.

01:06:15.259 --> 01:06:16.609
Okay, fine.

01:06:16.609 --> 01:06:19.829
How many of you got the other things?

01:06:19.829 --> 01:06:21.229
Both of them?

01:06:21.229 --> 01:06:23.489
Okay, well so you get the point.

01:06:23.489 --> 01:06:30.289
The point is--and it's a brilliant experiment,
it's an insanely brilliant experiment--that

01:06:30.289 --> 01:06:34.499
you're blind to a ridiculous degree.

01:06:34.499 --> 01:06:40.099
And your visual system and your brain are
constantly coping with the fact that you're

01:06:40.099 --> 01:06:41.099
blind.

01:06:41.099 --> 01:06:45.469
And you can kind of detect it to some degree
if you look at someone, but you pay attention

01:06:45.469 --> 01:06:49.729
to the things that surround them, you'll see
that--so, I'm looking at you.

01:06:49.729 --> 01:06:53.140
I can't see your nose for sure, it's gone.

01:06:53.140 --> 01:06:55.099
I can’t even see your face.

01:06:55.099 --> 01:06:57.549
I can see your glasses.

01:06:57.549 --> 01:06:58.819
I can't see any features in your face at all.

01:06:58.819 --> 01:06:59.819
Can you smile?

01:06:59.819 --> 01:07:06.280
No, the one behind.

01:07:06.280 --> 01:07:10.280
Well one of the things I've noticed when doing
this is that if you look here--look at my

01:07:10.280 --> 01:07:13.799
finger--but pay attention to my face, it's
really hard.

01:07:13.799 --> 01:07:17.049
Because normally when you pay attention you
move your eyes to what you're paying attention

01:07:17.049 --> 01:07:18.049
to.

01:07:18.049 --> 01:07:21.369
Okay so here's what you might see--the first
thing you might see is that you can't see

01:07:21.369 --> 01:07:23.210
my nose [student laughter].

01:07:23.210 --> 01:07:27.869
The second thing you might see is that you
can really see my eyes, especially if they

01:07:27.869 --> 01:07:30.059
move [uproar of student laughter].

01:07:30.059 --> 01:07:31.059
Weird eh?

01:07:31.059 --> 01:07:32.259
Isn't that weird?

01:07:32.259 --> 01:07:33.651
God, it's so weird.

01:07:33.651 --> 01:07:39.279
It's like all of a sudden these eyes pop up
and they're going back and forth like this.

01:07:39.279 --> 01:07:40.279
Yeah, so we'll try this too.

01:07:40.279 --> 01:07:44.499
So look at my finger again.

01:07:44.499 --> 01:07:45.650
You can also see teeth.

01:07:45.650 --> 01:07:46.900
Well, why?

01:07:46.900 --> 01:07:50.740
Well, if you're looking here and there's something
there with eyes and teeth, you should probably

01:07:50.740 --> 01:07:52.239
be able to see it right?

01:07:52.239 --> 01:07:56.370
So what that basically means is that you've
got this tiny little fovea, which is right

01:07:56.370 --> 01:08:02.099
in the centre of your retina, it's just densely
packed with cells and then those cells transmit

01:08:02.099 --> 01:08:06.640
information along the optic nerve, and they're
just connected like mad to cells in the visual

01:08:06.640 --> 01:08:07.839
cortex.

01:08:07.839 --> 01:08:12.199
But you don't have that much room, so you
can't connect all the cells in your retina

01:08:12.199 --> 01:08:14.970
to like 10,000 cells at the first level of
connection.

01:08:14.970 --> 01:08:18.220
You'd have to have a brain this big you know,
and you don't.

01:08:18.220 --> 01:08:23.500
So you get by, and part of the way you get
by is you're always moving your eyes around.

01:08:23.500 --> 01:08:26.359
And so it looks like you can see everything,
but you can't.

01:08:26.359 --> 01:08:31.810
In fact, out here, like if I'm looking at
you--now I can tell my hands are there, although

01:08:31.810 --> 01:08:37.060
if I didn't know they were hands I probably
couldn't figure it out--they're kinda blurry

01:08:37.060 --> 01:08:38.829
I would say.

01:08:38.829 --> 01:08:41.779
And they're in black and white, but I can't
tell that.

01:08:41.779 --> 01:08:45.380
So out here, who cares what colour it is.

01:08:45.380 --> 01:08:46.569
So you just don't see the colour.

01:08:46.569 --> 01:08:48.449
But you don't know that you don't see the
colour.

01:08:48.449 --> 01:08:52.819
Now of course, I can have my hands here, and
then I put them here--I can hardly see them--and

01:08:52.819 --> 01:08:54.619
then oop, they're gone.

01:08:54.619 --> 01:08:56.630
They're so gone it's like they don't even
exist.

01:08:56.630 --> 01:09:00.880
It's not even--because you'd think in some
sense that what is behind your head would

01:09:00.880 --> 01:09:05.049
be black, like when you close your eyes, that's
nothing.

01:09:05.049 --> 01:09:14.069
But no no, what you can't see behind your
head is so not there that it's literally--it

01:09:14.069 --> 01:09:17.440
isn't even not there, that's how gone it is.

01:09:17.440 --> 01:09:24.510
Okay, so part of the way your eyes work is
that the periphery is attached to devices,

01:09:24.510 --> 01:09:29.239
in some sense, and some of them detect lines
and some of them detect movement.

01:09:29.239 --> 01:09:30.739
You remember Jurassic Park?

01:09:30.739 --> 01:09:32.000
"Don't move.

01:09:32.000 --> 01:09:34.590
The dinosaur can't see you unless you move."

01:09:34.590 --> 01:09:37.320
It's like, yeah, frogs are like that.

01:09:37.320 --> 01:09:38.870
And cats are like that to some degree.

01:09:38.870 --> 01:09:41.020
They can really see things move this way.

01:09:41.020 --> 01:09:45.900
Partly because their eyes are slitted, makes
it easier for them to see things moving like

01:09:45.900 --> 01:09:46.900
this.

01:09:46.900 --> 01:09:47.900
They're not so good at things moving like
this.

01:09:47.900 --> 01:09:50.869
Well, it's because they chase mice, right.

01:09:50.869 --> 01:09:55.190
Mice will--most mice, except ones that hop
[student laughter]--they move this way, and

01:09:55.190 --> 01:09:57.420
so cats are specialized for that.

01:09:57.420 --> 01:10:02.860
And so you're also specialized to see teeth
and eyes because you want to know if something's

01:10:02.860 --> 01:10:05.810
looking at you, and you want to know if it
has teeth.

01:10:05.810 --> 01:10:12.010
And so what that means in part is that the
surrounding tissue in your eyes reports to

01:10:12.010 --> 01:10:16.960
some degree to your visual cortex, which is
what you use to actually have conscious vision

01:10:16.960 --> 01:10:20.810
with, but a lot of it just dumps down into
some of the brain areas we haven't talked

01:10:20.810 --> 01:10:21.810
about.

01:10:21.810 --> 01:10:26.980
Not necessarily directly down to the hypothalamus,
although I suspect it has inputs there, I

01:10:26.980 --> 01:10:28.070
just don't know.

01:10:28.070 --> 01:10:33.739
But your eyes talk to your spinal cord directly,
so that you can see something snake-like,

01:10:33.739 --> 01:10:35.030
for example, and jump.

01:10:35.030 --> 01:10:38.470
Just like you jump if your hand touches a
hot stove.

01:10:38.470 --> 01:10:41.340
You don't even see the snake, it's like snake-jump.

01:10:41.340 --> 01:10:44.341
And the reason for that is if you wait around
to see the snake, it's like it's bitten you

01:10:44.341 --> 01:10:45.900
15 times and you're dead.

01:10:45.900 --> 01:10:48.780
So you can't wait around and see this, like
"oh look.

01:10:48.780 --> 01:10:50.470
A snake."

01:10:50.470 --> 01:10:51.470
And then you jump.

01:10:51.470 --> 01:10:52.470
It's like, no.

01:10:52.470 --> 01:10:54.730
That doesn't work.

01:10:54.730 --> 01:11:00.290
Your body has conserved all these layers of
reflex and movement, all the way up to higher

01:11:00.290 --> 01:11:04.239
order voluntary movement, because otherwise
you just wouldn't be fast enough when something

01:11:04.239 --> 01:11:05.760
super fast happens.

01:11:05.760 --> 01:11:10.050
And then, the way your eyes are built, your
eyes can tell your body to jump.

01:11:10.050 --> 01:11:11.780
It's no vision, right.

01:11:11.780 --> 01:11:12.870
It's just pattern.

01:11:12.870 --> 01:11:14.620
Snake-pattern, jump-pattern.

01:11:14.620 --> 01:11:19.210
Snake-pattern, retinal-pattern, optic-nerve
pattern, brain-pattern, muscle-pattern.

01:11:19.210 --> 01:11:20.530
No vision.

01:11:20.530 --> 01:11:21.850
No seeing.

01:11:21.850 --> 01:11:23.380
Because you don't need the seeing.

01:11:23.380 --> 01:11:26.130
You just have to do pattern matching.

01:11:26.130 --> 01:11:29.401
It's a weird way of thinking about it, but
it's not much different than touching, you

01:11:29.401 --> 01:11:30.750
know.

01:11:30.750 --> 01:11:35.600
You can feel the pattern, you can build a
three-dimensional representation of it, you

01:11:35.600 --> 01:11:39.120
can really see well with your eyes--it's like
the idea that blind people can't see is a

01:11:39.120 --> 01:11:40.130
really dopey idea.

01:11:40.130 --> 01:11:41.690
They can't see colour.

01:11:41.690 --> 01:11:44.510
And they're not very good at detecting things
in the distance.

01:11:44.510 --> 01:11:48.310
But they have a visual world, it's just built
out of things that don't have colour.

01:11:48.310 --> 01:11:53.320
You know perfectly well that that's the case
because I can hand you an object and you could

01:11:53.320 --> 01:11:57.070
fiddle around with it behind your back and
after some fiddling around you'd know exactly

01:11:57.070 --> 01:11:58.620
what it looked like.

01:11:58.620 --> 01:12:02.239
And you know you might pull it out here and
say , well I didn't know it was blue.

01:12:02.239 --> 01:12:06.240
It's like, yeah okay, you didn't know it was
blue, but you still saw it.

01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:09.710
And you get pretty god-damn good vision with
your ears.

01:12:09.710 --> 01:12:13.159
So there are kids who're blind who've learned
to echolocate.

01:12:13.159 --> 01:12:17.500
So they go click-click-click, they don't make
that noise exactly, but they do click [clicking

01:12:17.500 --> 01:12:21.030
sounds] and they can detect the clicks bouncing
off of things.

01:12:21.030 --> 01:12:23.960
And they can do that well enough, not only
to walk, but some of them can actually ride

01:12:23.960 --> 01:12:25.210
bikes.

01:12:25.210 --> 01:12:29.920
It's like, hey that's impressive, man.

01:12:29.920 --> 01:12:34.880
Yeah, there's one kid--I saw a kid on youtube
who's blind who could shoot baskets, yeah,

01:12:34.880 --> 01:12:37.360
which is--that's impressive, man.

01:12:37.360 --> 01:12:43.440
But my point is that a lot of the neural tissue
is--it'll take whatever input it gets.

01:12:43.440 --> 01:12:49.170
So if you're born blind, your auditory systems
will invade your visual system.

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:52.239
And then you have twice as much brain devoted
to your hearing.

01:12:52.239 --> 01:12:55.270
So, and then you know, you can use that quite
effectively.

01:12:55.270 --> 01:12:59.820
So I think we're probably pretty much out
of time.

01:12:59.820 --> 01:13:03.159
One thing I want to tell you before we go
on to the next part though is that--so now

01:13:03.159 --> 01:13:08.429
we've sort of built up the idea of these underlying
motivational systems.

01:13:08.429 --> 01:13:14.909
And now you can kind of see how they work,
so you might say to yourself, why didn't you

01:13:14.909 --> 01:13:18.880
see the gorilla and the curtain and the person
leaving.

01:13:18.880 --> 01:13:21.659
It's really complicated, it's really complicated.

01:13:21.659 --> 01:13:27.990
Well, the first answer is, you did what I
asked.

01:13:27.990 --> 01:13:29.710
And then you might ask, well why?

01:13:29.710 --> 01:13:30.710
Why did you do what I asked?

01:13:30.710 --> 01:13:32.610
I mean you missed the damn gorilla, right.

01:13:32.610 --> 01:13:35.670
It's like, obviously that wasn't a very good
decision.

01:13:35.670 --> 01:13:38.110
So you think, well why did you do what I asked?

01:13:38.110 --> 01:13:43.500
And the answer would be--well, it's sort of
like the Milgram obedience experiment except

01:13:43.500 --> 01:13:45.610
at a way lower level.

01:13:45.610 --> 01:13:48.260
It's like, we've got an agreement going on
here.

01:13:48.260 --> 01:13:54.300
You guys are all very sophisticated and complex
game players, otherwise you couldn't sit here

01:13:54.300 --> 01:13:56.120
peacefully for an hour and a half.

01:13:56.120 --> 01:13:59.300
So you're very well socialized, all of you.

01:13:59.300 --> 01:14:05.350
And so that means you've organized all your
motivations into a hierarchy, and that hierarchy

01:14:05.350 --> 01:14:06.660
is guiding your behaviour here.

01:14:06.660 --> 01:14:10.491
It might be something like, well we need to
learn something and hypothetically, you come

01:14:10.491 --> 01:14:11.590
here and you learn something.

01:14:11.590 --> 01:14:13.650
And so you're playing that out.

01:14:13.650 --> 01:14:19.989
And because that's the motivational state
that you're in, when I ask you to do something,

01:14:19.989 --> 01:14:21.410
you're just going to do it.

01:14:21.410 --> 01:14:29.690
Well then, what's so cool is that as soon
as you decide to point your attention at something,

01:14:29.690 --> 01:14:32.230
what you see in the world radically transforms.

01:14:32.230 --> 01:14:36.840
So I say, well count the basketballs and all
of a sudden all you can see are basketballs.

01:14:36.840 --> 01:14:39.130
And you think, no no, I see--I'm not blind.

01:14:39.130 --> 01:14:42.380
It's like well, you're pretty damn blind.

01:14:42.380 --> 01:14:48.840
And so, that's partly how you can understand
how motivation is a sub-personality.

01:14:48.840 --> 01:14:51.060
Like it has its whole sets of perceptions
and values.

01:14:51.060 --> 01:14:56.179
It's like, all of a sudden, I set you up in
a little motivated state--it was a complicated

01:14:56.179 --> 01:14:59.380
one--but it was just like, here's a task.

01:14:59.380 --> 01:15:03.780
And so you're all motivated, you're going
to do the task properly, for god only knows

01:15:03.780 --> 01:15:09.090
how many reasons, and then all of a sudden,
all that exists in the world are basketballs.

01:15:09.090 --> 01:15:17.820
And so, it's as if your goal-directed attention
has transformed the phenomenology of the world.

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:23.050
And that's something that's really cool because
one of the things it implies is that what

01:15:23.050 --> 01:15:26.200
you see of the world depends on what you're
aiming at.

01:15:26.200 --> 01:15:28.890
And in a real sense--this isn't some trivial
sense.

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:33.739
It means like literally what you're aiming
at--I'm going to count the basketballs--it

01:15:33.739 --> 01:15:36.510
means, well you're not noticing the gorillas.

01:15:36.510 --> 01:15:39.969
So one of the things that you might ask yourself,
and this is partly what the phenomenologists

01:15:39.969 --> 01:15:43.989
were on about is, what're you aiming at?

01:15:43.989 --> 01:15:49.340
And what is it that what you're aiming at
allows you to see, and what is it that what

01:15:49.340 --> 01:15:51.860
you're aiming at blinds you to.

01:15:51.860 --> 01:15:55.110
And it blinds you to a lot more than it allows
you to see.

01:15:55.110 --> 01:15:58.699
And so another thing that you can think about
is like, if everything you see makes you angry

01:15:58.699 --> 01:16:05.860
and bitter and resentful, you might think,
well hmm, what exactly am I aiming at that's

01:16:05.860 --> 01:16:08.550
making the world organize itself around me
in that manner?

01:16:08.550 --> 01:16:14.250
And that would be a complex, for a Jungian
perspective.

01:16:14.250 --> 01:16:15.579
A complex would do that.

01:16:15.579 --> 01:16:20.000
So it's a set of values, they're sort of economist,
they inhabit you, it's a sub-personality,

01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:23.199
it's quite complex, it's got something it's
aiming at.

01:16:23.199 --> 01:16:27.060
You might not even know what that is because
you're acting it out instead of understanding

01:16:27.060 --> 01:16:28.060
it.

01:16:28.060 --> 01:16:32.860
And because you're inside of it, it lays the
world out for you in a particular way that

01:16:32.860 --> 01:16:37.679
makes the world look like it's showing you
that what you think is right.

01:16:37.679 --> 01:16:44.300
But if you're--what do they say-- to the man
with a hammer, everything looks like a nail.

01:16:44.300 --> 01:16:48.070
And that's one of the things that's so damn
cool about that experiment.

01:16:48.070 --> 01:16:49.590
It's like, really?

01:16:49.590 --> 01:16:51.199
I'm really that blind?

01:16:51.199 --> 01:16:56.880
I can miss something that radical just when
I'm involved in some dopey task like counting

01:16:56.880 --> 01:17:01.000
basketballs it makes me so blind that it's
almost beyond comprehension.

01:17:01.000 --> 01:17:06.280
Well, so in some ways you skip between different
states of blindness.

01:17:06.280 --> 01:17:08.520
A lot of them are biologically motivated.

01:17:08.520 --> 01:17:12.889
And you turn those into a hierarchy and that's
sort of your higher value system and then

01:17:12.889 --> 01:17:17.230
that thing points you at something, and the
whole world lays itself out around that.

01:17:17.230 --> 01:17:21.860
And the way it does it is divides the world
into irrelevant things--and so those would've

01:17:21.860 --> 01:17:26.489
been the curtains in this particular example,
or the gorilla or even the player in black

01:17:26.489 --> 01:17:30.750
who left the game--irrelevant, because it
didn't interfere with you counting.

01:17:30.750 --> 01:17:34.010
So what you look at makes most of the world
irrelevant.

01:17:34.010 --> 01:17:36.800
It makes some things , things that serve you.

01:17:36.800 --> 01:17:39.110
And that makes you feel positive emotion.

01:17:39.110 --> 01:17:41.489
And it makes other things things that get
in your way.

01:17:41.489 --> 01:17:43.680
And that's what makes negative emotions.

01:17:43.680 --> 01:17:48.030
And so you've got the hypothalamic systems
that point you in biological directions.

01:17:48.030 --> 01:17:54.600
You organize those, and then, as you're approaching
the things you're aiming at, the positive

01:17:54.600 --> 01:17:58.349
emotion systems tell you you're on track and
the negative emotion systems tell you when

01:17:58.349 --> 01:17:59.750
something's interfering.

01:17:59.750 --> 01:18:03.280
And those are actually different neurological
systems.

01:18:03.280 --> 01:18:05.010
So they're not in the hypothalamus.

01:18:05.010 --> 01:18:07.699
The positive emotion system is in some degree.

01:18:07.699 --> 01:18:10.550
But the negative emotion system, that evolved
later.

01:18:10.550 --> 01:18:11.550
And there's a couple of them.

01:18:11.550 --> 01:18:14.170
There's one that produces pain, we'll talk
about it more.

01:18:14.170 --> 01:18:16.570
And there's another one that produces anxiety.

01:18:16.570 --> 01:18:24.850
And so, now you kind of understand--roughly--the
relationship between motivation, perception,

01:18:24.850 --> 01:18:26.070
and emotion.

01:18:26.070 --> 01:18:29.010
They all act together.

01:18:29.010 --> 01:18:33.070
And they're all dependent in this weird way
on what you're after.

01:18:33.070 --> 01:18:36.770
So you can think for 50 years and you won't
get to the bottom of it.

01:18:36.770 --> 01:18:39.020
You know the Buddhists say that everything
is illusion.

01:18:39.020 --> 01:18:41.500
Maya, right, people live in illusion.

01:18:41.500 --> 01:18:44.449
Because what you're aiming at determines what
you see.

